LUA(1)                      User Commands                     LUA(1)

NAME
    lua - Lua 5.5 interpreter

SYNOPSIS
    lua [options] [script [arguments...]]

DESCRIPTION
    lua loads and runs Lua programs in source form or as chunks precompiled
    by luac. The arguments are strings in the global table arg, and they
    are passed to the script's main chunk. Without arguments lua behaves as
    lua -v -i when the standard input is a terminal and as lua - otherwise.

    The interactive prompt evaluates each line. An expression prints its
    values, and an incomplete statement continues on the secondary prompt
    until it is complete or a syntax error is found.

OPTIONS
    -e stat       run the statement stat
    -i            enter interactive mode after the script
    -l mod        require mod into the global mod
    -l g=mod      require mod into the global g
    -v            print the version
    -E            ignore the environment variables below
    -W            turn warnings on
    --            stop handling options
    -             stop handling options and run the standard input

INTERACTIVE EDITING
    Lines are edited with the keys of sh(1). The arrows move and recall
    history, Home/End and Ctrl-A/E move to the line ends, Ctrl-K, Ctrl-U
    and Ctrl-W erase, and Ctrl-R searches the history. Tab completes a
    global name, or a field after a dotted chain of names, so string.fo
    completes to string.format. Ctrl-C discards the line being edited, and
    Ctrl-D on an empty line ends the session. Each line of a statement
    enters the history on its own.

MODULES
    require finds Lua modules in /usr/share/lua/5.5 and in the current
    directory; there is no loading of C modules. The interpreter provides
    fs, sys, gui, audio and thread without a search.

ENVIRONMENT
    LUA_INIT, LUA_INIT_5_5  code run before the options and the script,
                            or @file to run a file
    LUA_PATH, LUA_PATH_5_5  the initial package.path
    HOME                    the directory of the history file

FILES
    $HOME/.lua_history      history of the interactive prompt, read at
                            the first prompt and written at exit
    /usr/share/lua/5.5      the module directory

EXIT STATUS
    lua exits with the status given to os.exit, else with 0 when no error
    occurred and 1 after an error. Errors at the interactive prompt do not
    end the session.

SEE ALSO
    luac(1), sh(1), the reference manual at lua.org
